{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Graph questions rarely say *graph*. They talk about islands, courses, flights, accounts or words. The skill is **recognition**: noticing that the problem is really things joined to other things, and that what's asked is one of a few standard moves."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Three questions to ask"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": true,
      "items": [
        "**What are the vertices, and what are the edges?** Cells and their neighbours, courses and prerequisites, words that differ by one letter, points and the cost to join them.",
        "**Directed? Weighted?** \"A before B\" is directed. \"Costs 5\" is weighted. Plain neighbours are neither.",
        "**What's being asked?** Can I reach it, how many pieces, in what order, how few steps, how cheap — each answer points to a pattern below."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The 12 patterns, from easiest to hardest"
    },
    {
      "type": "drawing",
      "src": "/drawings/cbafe66c-55a6-4b71-86ce-8d68096b6c62.svg",
      "alt": "Twelve cards in three columns, ordered from easiest (green) to hardest (red): traverse and count, multi-source BFS, start from the border, cycles and two colours, topological sort, BFS shortest path, union-find, Dijkstra, Bellman-Ford, spanning tree, Floyd-Warshall and bridges, each with a two-word signal.",
      "width": 842,
      "height": 537,
