    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": true,
      "items": [
        "**Traverse and count** — *\"how many islands / groups / provinces\"*, *\"fill the region\"*. Walk each unvisited piece with DFS or BFS and count the walks. Flood Fill, Number of Islands, Number of Provinces, Clone Graph.",
        "**Multi-source BFS** — *\"how long until it spreads everywhere\"*, *\"distance to the nearest X\"*. Start BFS from **all** sources at once. Rotting Oranges, 01 Matrix.",
        "**Start from the border** — *\"regions that don't touch the edge\"*, *\"cells that can reach the ocean\"*. Walk inwards from the border instead of outwards from every cell. Surrounded Regions, Pacific Atlantic Water Flow.",
        "**Cycles and two-colouring** — *\"is there a loop\"*, *\"split into two groups with no conflict\"*. DFS or BFS that remembers parents or colours. Detect a Cycle, Is Graph Bipartite?",
        "**Topological sort** — *\"prerequisites\"*, *\"order of tasks\"*, *\"dependencies\"*. Repeatedly take what has nothing left to wait for. Course Schedule I and II, Alien Dictionary.",
        "**BFS shortest path** — *\"minimum number of steps / moves / changes\"* when every move costs the same. Shortest Path in Binary Matrix, Word Ladder.",
        "**Union-Find** — *\"are these connected\"*, *\"merge accounts\"*, *\"which edge makes a cycle\"*, especially as edges arrive one by one. Redundant Connection, Accounts Merge.",
        "**Dijkstra** — *\"shortest / cheapest / fastest\"* when edges have **different, non-negative** costs. Network Delay Time, Path With Minimum Effort.",
        "**Shortest path with a limit** — *\"at most k stops\"*, or negative costs. Bellman-Ford relaxes every edge a fixed number of times. Cheapest Flights Within K Stops.",
        "**Minimum spanning tree** — *\"connect all points at the minimum total cost\"*. Prim or Kruskal. Min Cost to Connect All Points.",
        "**All-pairs shortest paths** — *\"distance between every pair\"*, with a small number of vertices. Floyd-Warshall. Find the City With the Smallest Number of Neighbors.",
        "**Bridges** — *\"which connections, if cut, disconnect the network\"*. Tarjan's low-link DFS. Critical Connections in a Network."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Choosing the algorithm"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Reach, count or copy** — BFS or DFS, **O(V + E)**.",
        "**Fewest edges** (every edge costs the same) — BFS, **O(V + E)**.",
        "**Cheapest path, costs ≥ 0** — Dijkstra with a heap, **O(E log V)**.",
        "**Cheapest path with at most k edges, or negative costs** — Bellman-Ford, **O(k · E)** or O(V · E).",
        "**Order with dependencies** — topological sort, **O(V + E)**.",
        "**Groups that keep merging** — Union-Find, nearly **O(1)** per merge or check.",
        "**Cheapest way to connect everything** — Prim or Kruskal, **O(E log V)**.",
        "**Every pair at once, V up to a few hundred** — Floyd-Warshall, **O(V³)**.",
        "**Critical edges** — Tarjan's DFS, **O(V + E)**."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Read every new problem with these in mind: name the vertices and edges first, and the pattern usually names itself. The rest of the course takes the patterns one at a time, in exactly this order."
    }
  ],
